Z||000000||GILLICK||05-28-2001||10:16 PM||||Hello everyone,<P> I am new to this forum, but everyone seems great.  I am contemplating doing an engine swap.  I am more concerned about quality and "everyday" driveability.  I have it narrowed down to 3800 sc or V8.  I don't mean to start a trash session but would love to hear any stories concerning and shops and their installations.  Ex. Archie, ACE, any others out there.<P>    I may consider having my mechanic (local St. Louis) install a kit just to have someone more knowledgeable than me, and close, know what has been done.<P>Thanks in advance<P><P>------------------<BR>John D. Gillick<BR>jgillick@swbell.net||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||05-28-2001||10:31 PM||||This is what I would like to try. <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/</A> <P>v8 conversion from a frt wheel drive engine.  I think would still make an affordable daily driver.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jay||05-28-2001||11:31 PM||||Welcome to the board! I suggest looking at a few different places such as: <A HREF="http://www.thefieroshop.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefieroshop.com</A> <BR>Bubbajoe's page looks good too.  Seems to know his stuff.<BR>My page has info and photos of my Northstar conversion.<BR>Good Luck<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gangster.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Archie||05-29-2001||12:51 AM||||Don't pay any attention to those guys,<BR>They don't measure up no matter how hard they trys.<P>After you've looked at the rest, <BR>Go for the best  <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/</A> <P>Hey, I'm a poet and don't know-it.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||05-29-2001||12:59 AM||||Don't bastardize your Fiero with a SBC, go for the 3800SC.  Check out <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>  for everything you need.  <P>Check out <A HREF="http://www.nytmare.org" TARGET=_blank>www.nytmare.org</A>  for a V8 testimonial...<P><p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 05-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cozmo||05-29-2001||09:06 AM||||What tough choices <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><BR>I want one of each...Hmmm let's see now...<BR>-SBC Archie (engine choices are almost limitless)<BR>-Northstar<BR>-4.9 Caddy<BR>-3800SC or N/A<BR>-3.4 DOHC<BR>-3.4 turbo<BR>-SD4<BR>-Quad 4<BR>OK I'm up to 8 cars and counting...<BR><B>C02M0</B><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000006||Hartz||05-29-2001||09:27 AM||||Black88GT - just curious - why would stuffing a SBC in a Fiero be "bastardizing" it, but a 3800SC wouldn't?  Seriously - I am curious as to why so many hold this opinion.<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000007||Mr.Frosty||05-29-2001||10:46 AM||||I've been told that here in this state it is illegal to do a V8 swap because of our new strict emissions laws.Has anyone heard of this? Something to do about going from a 6 to an 8 cyl.<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top-Sold <BR>85 GT Turbo-project car||||reg||5
Z||000008||BV MotorSports||05-29-2001||12:00 PM||||I would go with a 3.8 swap. Its much easier and WAY cheaper. Don't let Archie or the other V8 guys corrupt you. They know as I well as I that while v8 swaps are "cool" and give that "wow" factor they are a HUGE compromise.<P>Added Weight<BR>Cutting/trimming of engine bay/sheetmetal /chassis<BR>Some have the water pump sticking out in the fenderwell!<BR>Try to change the drive belts...OUCH<BR>Cooling concerns<BR>Custom parts from one or 2 small suppliers<BR>(how long are they going to be around?)<P>Theres a lot to it.. This isn't fingering out Archie but all the v8 swappers. So Don't think I am bashing v8 swaps. I have done them and wouldn't own one for myself. Been there done that and didn't like it. I haven't done a Northstar but it has to be about the same.<P>3.8 will give you way more for the money. No cutting required and needs just a few custom parts (vrs a lot for a v8). Will definitely have a factory appearance.<P>You choice, you money, spend it wisely.<P>Steven<BR><P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 93mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
Z||000009||Hartz||05-29-2001||01:01 PM||||Does anyone know how much a 3800SC weighs?  <P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000010||Bazooka||05-29-2001||01:42 PM||||   I agree with 88GT and am speaking from experience. Don't waste your money on a V8 conversion, go with the 3800 SC. I've had to postpone using my car for 2 years after the V8 conversiuon because of problems that 'nickel and dime you to death' from the first day. I've gone thru most of the disasters that are possible. Blown Fuses, Engine Fires(Brand New Engine), Broken Belts out on the road, Wheels Flying off from improper reassembly at balljoint, Exhaust Manifold Leaks every time it came out of the shop, Intake Leaks at fuel injection components, Improperlt sized fuel pump, Blown Transmission rebuilt 5 times by the same shop and still unresolved (Just an inadequate application, Miserable Gas Mileage (8 mpg), Bad access to engine parts (to remove valve covers the cradle has to be dropped) as well as countless other costly items. This V8 conversion was very expensive and just think if you had to double that cost just to get it running like a normal street machine??? <BR>  See my car at the following website<BR>    <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <P>It looks clean now, but don't be misled. It was a long road to this point. It's not worth it. Go with a 3800SC.<P>   <BR>**************************************<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>Don't bastardize your Fiero with a SBC, go for the 3800SC.  Check out <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>  for everything you need.  <P>Check out <A HREF="http://www.nytmare.org" TARGET=_blank>www.nytmare.org</A>  for a V8 testimonial...<P>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 05-29-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Hartz||05-29-2001||02:37 PM||||Bazooka - No flames intended, but how many of your problems were due to bad installation, and how many were specific to the fact that it was a V8?  Wheels flying off?!?  How is that related to it being a V8?<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000012||elfiero||05-29-2001||04:00 PM||||I've done 3800,3800S/C,&SBC swaps.I presently<BR>own a SBC 5spd 87GT-overall I think the best<BR>is a regular 3800 for non-mechanic owners.It<BR>is the easist to work on and the most reliable.I think it is plenty quick unless<BR>your gonna streetrace it!||||reg||6
Z||000013||Bazooka||05-29-2001||04:24 PM||||Hartz,<BR>   The problems were created by the builder and installer who is an expert at V8 Fiero Conversions. I wanted only the best materials and equipment for this Fiero. I've owned it since 1987 and felt an attachment to it. I've done a dozen rebuilding jobs myself (Buick 6 and 8, Mustang 4 and 6's, Cadillac Eldorado, Oldsmobile 350, Mercury Capri, etc.)but thought the pros could do it more efficiently. Wrong! The first misconception is that the Fiero transmissions will handle 400-450 HP - right-  .... Wrong!!! That is the biggest mistake you can. Even the performance Transmission shops say just about any V8 has too much torque for the Fiero transes. Call 'Roadmasters' in Schaumburg, Illinois and see what Bill Andrews has to say about the V8 in a Fiero. 225 HP is max according to many. I have had ASE certification in previous years and do know something about this. The V8 conversion was designed and installed by the same place and all I did was roll in and out of the shop for a year and a half. The idea of more horsepower was the main selling point of the builder and I was assured that it was a reliable design, that is until I got the car back. My Fiero was in the shop for 6 months for the original conversion and every time after that (5) it stayed there for 3-5 months each time for the returns. Plus the interior became filthy with shop dust and I even got free body damage from someone in the shops' joyriding. I guess that's as good as it gets. The point is that the Fiero wasn't made for a V8 and no matter what the novelty seems to be it it will fail in the end. Again the 3800 SC has had good feedback.  <BR>*******************************************<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hartz:<BR><B>Bazooka - No flames intended, but how many of your problems were due to bad installation, and how many were specific to the fact that it was a V8?  Wheels flying off?!?  How is that related to it being a V8?<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Hartz||05-29-2001||04:40 PM||||Bazooka - I hear you.  I did the same with my turbo car and had a fairly similar situation.  It took longer, was more expensive, got a few scratches in the paint, didn't like the overall quality of the job, lots of little repairs had to be made, had to have a flatbed bring it home twice, etc.  I think the lesson for all is if you want something done right, do it yourself.<P>I've never had a chance to build a 3800 car - or ride in one for that matter - but would love to do so someday.  Maybe when the turbo car cooks itself... Hmmmmmm.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  But I was just curious about everyone's experiences, because I hear lots of people bashing V8 conversions and I didn't think they're so bad, so I'm really curious about 3800s, considering everyone talks those up so much.  <P><BR>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||88formula||05-29-2001||05:09 PM||||In the last Hot Rod magazine there is an article on Phil's SC 3800 Formula. In there they say Phil has driven 25,000 trouble free miles. I have driven a five speed SC 3800 fiero and believe me it has more than enough torque and horsepower to satisfy most anyone.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Bazooka||05-29-2001||05:32 PM||||   I envy Phil and his 3800. It sounds like he's very satisfied with it. But also, it seems that you can boost the HP even higher with the newer versions of the 3800 and a larger pulley for the supercharger. That may allow you to get like 275-300 HP and match it with 440T6 that's engineered to handle it. Heck, In the Hot Rod article I think they mentioned that Phils car was turning mid 13's in the quarter mile. Thats fast for a street car, and faster than my V8. Design One in Colorado, Bob Steiger in Paxton, Illinois and Chris Moore in Indiana are just a few knowledgeable guys on the 3800. <BR>************************************<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B>In the last Hot Rod magazine there is an article on Phil's SC 3800 Formula. In there they say Phil has driven 25,000 trouble free miles. I have driven a five speed SC 3800 fiero and believe me it has more than enough torque and horsepower to satisfy most anyone.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Jay||05-29-2001||05:35 PM||||I've gotta agree on some points.  The 3800 is an easier engine to install, including wiring.  As for the V8 being a headache, I've found just the opposite.  I've got a 97 Northstar engine in my 86 GT which I did myself (for the most part) in my garage.  There were no custom parts to be fabricated, just modified (ie. 1/2 shafts, craddle, spindles, brakes).  My car averages about 20mpg with the performance shift select on.  Don't know what it would be if it was left in normal driving mode.  Yes there are some difficulties, like changing the oil.  The filler cap is on the firewall side now.  But that is a small price to pay.  The spark plugs are easier to access than a stock V6.  The rear brakes are now larger and vented.  As for the tranny, I used the one that came with the engine, so no problems there.  The fit of the engine looks like it belongs in the engine bay.  I spent less then $10 000 Canadian on the engine conversion, doing it myself, and that was the first swap of any kind that I've done.  That included wiring, suspension (lowered 2"), brake upgrade, intake, radiator, gauges, rims, rubber, new leather seats, basically everything.  I've never been stranded and it has never overheated.  Reliability personified.  But I do like the 3800 conversion as well.  For me it was a toss up between the two.  I wanted something different, so I went with the Northstar.  Whatever you do, ask a ton of questions, ask others who have the conversion kit how it is, and never pay for anything unless you get it up front!  On the forum you'll find a wealth of info.  Bazooka, I was thinking the same thing that Harz was.  So are the problems resolved now?  I don't agree with you that the V8 is doomed for failure.  There are many out there with similar experiences as mine.  Chris Moore, and John Norman have had their Northstar cars for years.  Archie has had SBC since the beginning of time (sorry Archie but that's what it feels like <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)  Bottom line, 3800 or N* or SBC can be done and can be reliable, it's a matter of preferance.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gangster.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Bazooka||05-29-2001||07:09 PM||||Jay and list,<BR> I do agree that any conversion can be successful if thought out and done carefully. I'm sure that the Northstar is another success along with the 3800 according to owners I've talked to. But I can't agree with the SBC in any Fiero as being reliable. I've had costlier and more frequent breakages with the Chevy 350 than with all of the previous 78 cars that I've owned over the years. If mild V8's are used then what is the purpose? You won't see any SBC V8's in a Fiero at the dragstrips, either. Why?  Because breakage is costly and it would be obvious in a competitive atmosphere as to what is and isn't reliable. <BR>**************************************** <BR>[QUOTE]Originally posted by Jay:<BR>[B]I've gotta agree on some points.  The 3800 is an easier engine to install, including wiring.  As for the V8 being a headache, I've found just the opposite.  I've got a 97 Northstar engine in my 86 GT which I did myself (for the most part) in my garage.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Hartz||05-29-2001||08:41 PM||||13.3 at the quarter.  What's the fun of building it if you can't drive it?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'd drive my SBC V8 anywhere.  Again, I think it is how you build it that will determine reliability.  Just my $.02.  I'd agree that you can't really go wrong with any of the choices.  <P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||12
